What:		/sys/class/leds/asus::keyboard/
What:		/sys/class/leds/asus::lightbar/
What:		/sys/class/leds/asus::logo/
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Marco Scardovi <scardracs@disroot.org>
Contact:	Denis Benato <denis.benato@linux.dev>
Description:	ASUS Aura keyboard, chassis lightbar, and lid-logo Dynamic
		Lighting nodes. Each node accepts its own effect, palette, and
		(where supported) direct RGB writes. Firmware effects, including
		static color, are committed with Aura 0xb3/0xb5/0xb4 so the
		controller keeps them across reboot. On asus::keyboard,
		brightness is the firmware backlight dimmer and does not gate
		the zone; enabled gates the zone with the power command.
		asus::lightbar has no separate firmware dimmer: zone on/off is
		``enabled`` only (brightness is not a meaningful intensity
		control). asus::logo is registered when Aura discovery reports
		a lid-logo region (probe byte 13 bit 0); it owns Aura zone 0x05
		and independent logo power bits. Lid and rear-glow protocol
		bytes follow the keyboard ``power_states`` bits (no dedicated
		lid/rear LED nodes). Direct RGB may be backed by HID LampArray
		(Usage Page 0x59) when Aura 0xBC cannot address the lightbar
		independently. The lightbar and logo nodes are absent when the
		device has no matching region.

What:		/sys/class/leds/asus::slash/
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Marco Scardovi <scardracs@disroot.org>
Contact:	Denis Benato <denis.benato@linux.dev>
Description:	ASUS ROG Slash Dynamic Lighting node. ``effect`` /
		``effect_index`` select Slash modes (Static, Bounce, …).
		``speed`` maps the former Slash interval (0-5). ``enabled``
		gates on/off via the Slash options packet; ``brightness`` is
		the firmware intensity (0-255) and does not gate the bar.
		``power_states`` supports boot/sleep/shutdown persistence
		(protocol 0xd3 a0/a1/a4). Lid-closed and battery triggers are
		not exposed through Dynamic Lighting power_states. On Aura
		keyboards Slash uses feature report 0x5e. USB ID 0x193b is
		shared with AniMe Matrix; the driver claims it for Slash only
		when feature report 0x5e size and DMI board discrimination
		select Slash rather than AniMe. The node is gated on
		CONFIG_LEDS_CLASS_DYNAMIC.

What:		/sys/class/leds/asus::anime/
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Marco Scardovi <scardracs@disroot.org>
Contact:	Denis Benato <denis.benato@linux.dev>
Description:	ASUS AniMe Matrix Dynamic Lighting node (USB 0x193b when
		discriminated as AniMe). ``brightness`` is the firmware
		display level (0-3). ``enabled`` maps to display on/off.
		``effect`` selects ``direct`` streaming or programs one
		per-power-state builtin slot. Builtin names are prefixed with
		the slot (``boot_``, ``awake_``, ``sleep_``, ``shutdown_``);
		writing one updates only that slot's firmware stage choice.
		``power_states`` gates the firmware powersave-animation engine
		for those slots without clearing the stored stage selection.
		``direct_buffer`` accepts one intensity byte per LED;
		``led_count`` matches the board layout (GA401, GA402, GU604,
		G635L, G835L). Unknown AniMe boards use a GA402-like
		three-pane layout.

What:		/sys/class/leds/asus::xbox_logo/
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Marco Scardovi <scardracs@disroot.org>
Contact:	Denis Benato <denis.benato@linux.dev>
Description:	Xbox Guide button LED on ROG Xbox Ally / Xbox Ally X
		(``asus::xbox_logo``). Dynamic Lighting exposes ``effect`` /
		``effect_index`` and ``speed`` via Ally MCU commands
		d1 21 (effect+speed) and d1 20 (brightness 0-3). ``enabled``
		gates the Guide LED without changing the last effect.
		Present only on Ally devices that advertise Guide LED support
		(or Xbox Ally DMI).
